<section class="story-section" id="story">
  <div class="s-label reveal">Why I Built This</div>
  <h2 class="s-title reveal stagger-1">Why It Took Me Too Long<br><em>To Find It</em></h2>
  <div class="story-grid">
    <div class="story-aside reveal">"The shift came when I stopped trying to fix finished garments and started asking a different question."</div>
    <div class="story-body reveal stagger-2">
      <p>If you have ever spent weeks on a sweater, tried it on for the first time, and felt your stomach drop... you know exactly where this story starts.</p>
      <p>I was there. Following the pattern to the letter. Counting every stitch. Swatching. Blocking. Doing everything right. And still — the armholes sat in the wrong place. The bust pulled. The waist looked like a paper bag.</p>
      <p>I tried the obvious fixes. Made the whole thing a size bigger. Added a few stitches here and there. Watched hours of tutorials that each gave me a different answer. Nothing worked. Every attempt either created a new problem or made the old one worse.</p>
      <p><strong>The real issue was not my skill. It was not my yarn. And it was definitely not my body.</strong> The problem was that I was treating a flat, mathematical pattern as if it were already shaped for a real human being — and it simply was not.</p>
      <p>The shift came when I stopped trying to fix finished garments and started asking a different question: what if I could know the fit was right before I ever got that far?</p>
      <p>That question led me to study how professional designers actually approach fit. I tested it. I refined it. I turned it into a step-by-step system that any maker can follow, even if math is not your strong suit and you have never modified a pattern before. The result is the <strong>Perfect Fit Formula.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
